 {:seon.task/name "a-model-render-draws-each-turns-diff"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: \"This needs to be entirely data driven. The model is just doing a recursive walk of the hiccup that was previously generted for the agents page. We need to have model render functions that build the context for the agent. That' how the \"conversation\" becomes the assistant, user, system messages, tool calls, etc. Same system so the rendrer functions also get a current db they can query to do additional data pulls or to selectively show data like the diffs for example. But it's a render function that takes in a spec that we recognize from the hiccup. Same as the layout stuff wasn't a special layout function. NO NEW SYSTEMS.\" And on each turn: the agent gets the db at the start of its turn, diffed from its previous turn; a turn removed in compaction is no longer drawn, and the context is drawn again from what the db holds. Nothing drawn is stored; a cache is never relied on; a render whose code changed draws the data again with the new code. Words: Clojure's and the libraries' (entity, attribute, value, ref, a point {:branch :commit :t} from Datahike's commit and t, hiccup, editscript's diff). So the per-turn diff is a spec placed in the conversation's hiccup for each turn, drawn by a model render that reads the db at that turn's two points; seon.turn/show-page! (a stored eval each turn) and seon.ctx.wire/request's appended call go. The text below is the earlier shape, kept for its measurements.

What's wrong, measured on a lab root at 7716d15518 (worktree recursive-ctx, through bin/mcp): Seon stores the hiccup on the agent as evals, (my.render/render-ctx) whole and (my.render/render-changes) edits, a copy of derived data, with the base of the next diff in the walk's :shown, process memory. A restart sends the whole hiccup again (8 of 8 leading messages identical, then 3,534 chars, the same value as the first; 14,269 -> 18,609). Removing the whole one ((swap! ctx update :evals dissoc id), a db fact) leaves a 178-char diff against nothing. seon.ctx.wire/redo-tx exists only to repair those copies.

The change: nothing about the hiccup is stored. seon.render/wire, reading the conversation, places before each turn a call that is never recorded, its arguments the points it stands between, from each turn's :seon.model-call/basis (a required :commit) and the agent's branch: the first turn (my.render/hiccup point), whole; each later one (my.render/diff point0 point1), editscript's edits between the hiccup at the two points (seon.render/component-edits); a turn where nothing moved, none. request's never-recorded call path (the [:render] ref) becomes the only one. Pure: the same points and the same render code give the same bytes, and the agent can make the same call at its REPL. Compaction is removing records or turns: the remaining turns' points are the points, only a diff spanning a removed turn changes, and the cache busts from there. The request draws every point with today's render code, so a render that changes re-renders the history and busts the cache from turn 1, on purpose. Time travel to a point draws it with that point's render code (going back to an eval exists in seon.root; to verify: whether a process forked on that branch runs that commit's renders). A cache keyed by point and render code is an optimization; a miss gives the same bytes.

Measured: the diffs derived from consecutive basis commits equal the stored evals byte for byte over 7 turns, except where a render's code changed (drawn with today's code, as ruled). Cold, 42 turns: a fresh walk per point 375.7M ticks, 1.44 GB, 1.5 s; one walk moved forward 296.6M, of which seon.ctx/moved diffing consecutive commits is 229.3M for 19,985 datoms, 354 of each turn's ~410 being :seon.fn/stats rewritten on the branch every turn (with Optimization). To do with it: skip a turn whose moved datoms touch nothing the hiccup read (flush! answers {}); measure again after the stats leave the record.

Deletes: the stored hiccup evals and seon.turn/show-page!'s insert; my.render/render-ctx, render-changes, redo-changes; seon.ctx.wire/redo-tx, page-at, render-calls, render-eval?; seon.render/forms-moved where only redo used it, page-call, page-value, the walk's :shown; :seon.eval/page (dropped, no erase needed); the word page in these names; docs/render.md#a-redo-is-an-explicit-bust, and docs/render.md#the-render-is-an-eval rewritten; seon.agent-test/a-redo-is-drawn-from-the-page-as-of-each-t. seon.ctx.wire/at-t stays (seon.root uses it).

Proofs, red first: a restart with nothing moved sends nothing new, the request after it the one before plus the new eval; removing a turn changes only the diff that spans it; a render changed between two turns re-renders the history with the new code; the request built this way equals today's on a history with no render change; a cold build's cost at 10, 100 and 1,000 turns (seon.cost/proof).

Settled from the map of 2026-11-08 (Context, read-only over jolt/arm-content):
- The old hiccup's source: the live walk keeps the forms it drew at the last turn's point, keyed by that point (a cache, never the truth); with none (a restart, a cold read), the walk draws point k-1 and advances to point k. The diff is seon.render/page-edits over seon.render/forms-moved of the two, so forms-moved stays.
- The call the model reads, and the agent's REPL, is one function, (my.render/diff p0 p1), with p a point {:branch :commit :t}: the walk fills :seon.render/changes with it, and an agent calling it gets the same bytes. One function, two callers.
- The turn not yet recorded (seon.turn/context-block, the judge's first move): its point is passed to the render as an opt, since its call has no basis yet.
- The first turn is edits from nothing ([[id] :+ form], the diff's own vocabulary), so the separate whole value (parts-value, :seon.render/parts) goes.
- Build order, each green alone: (1) delete redo-changes, redo-tx, page-at and their tests; (2) add :seon.render/changes to seon.render/inputs and render!, filled once per pair of points, with its cost proof; (3) my.render/diff and the equality proof: on a history with no render change, the request built from derived diffs equals today's byte for byte; (4) in one commit, conversation-turn draws each turn's diff, show-page! and request's appended call go, the prompt and the page-eval tests move to points; (5) delete :shown, page-value, page-call, moved, render-calls, render-eval?, my.render/render-ctx and render-changes, :seon.eval/page, parts-value, the docs' \"The render is an eval\" and \"A redo is an explicit bust\". Rule 7's per-turn reach check reuses render-reach and queue-moved-renders!; the restart's range check waits on Optimization's fork work.

Steps 1-3 built (ebe6fc7581, 1f373dd800, 431a2d3b39) and reviewed by Context, 2026-11-08: the redo is gone, and my.render/diff answers right (equal to the stored page evals over four turns), but it costs the wrong thing. seon.ctx.wire/forms-between draws the whole layout, the conversation too, for each pair of points, and with nothing kept draws p0 cold then advances to p1: 1.4M to 13.5M ticks a pair at 10 to 80 evals against a 0.1M minimum, and a restart of T turns is T cold draws. The walk also keeps :diffs and :held beside :shown, a second cache. Redesign before step 4:
- One cache: the walk's diffs keyed by pair of points, {[p0 p1] edits}. Live, each turn fills its pair from what the walk already computes (seon.render/moved against :shown, as seon.render/page-value does today), so a live turn costs what moved, as now; :held goes.
- Cold (a restart, an outside read, an evicted pair): one model-only walk drawn at the first point and advanced through every basis point in order (seon.ctx/moved, seon.ctx/patch, flush!), filling every missing pair in one pass. Never a cold draw per pair. Floor, from Optimization (2026-11-08): the sum of the moved datoms over the history, since every request sends every turn's diff; about 56 moved datoms a turn since stats are written once a run (b72d9c52a4), and d/datoms-diff costs what changed on LMDB (0.33-1.36M for 21 datoms, flat in the index), so about 1M a pair and 40-50M for 42 turns, an estimate to measure first, on LMDB only (the :memory store walks the whole index). Rule 7's range check (skip a turn whose moved datoms touch no read range) lowers the constant only; it waits on Optimization's fork work.
- A walk with no way to draw a point is an error value, never an absent input.
- Measure first, then build: the advance's cost per turn on a lab root's real agent, 10 and 40 turns.

Built 2026-11-08 (helper 0abec1f379, reviewed by Context): one cache (:diffs), seon.ctx.wire/diffs-through (one walk across a chain of points), seon.ctx.wire/points-of (the turns' bases in order), a typed error for a walk that cannot draw a point. Context's fix: the first ask walks to the last turn (a request asks turn 1's pair first; walking only to the pair asked drew again each turn). Measured on a lab agent (LMDB, 10 turns): an advance 1.18M ticks of moved datoms plus 0.19M of patch; a cold draw 13.4M, 70% of it the request's search, now drawn only once its query has a vector (main 0b023d103b). Test world (:memory, which walks the whole index on a diff): about 590 calls and 5.3 MB a turn.
The live pair belongs in step 4, not show-page!: show-page! runs before the turn's basis is committed, so its edits are not the diff between bases. In step 4 the request's own walk is drawn at the turn's basis, and :shown holds the page at the turn before's: the pair [previous basis, basis] is page-edits of forms-moved of the two, stored in :diffs, costing what moved.

Owner, 2026-11-09 (stage 2b of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09): \"So it's not a spec that's embedded in the hiccup it's data that matches a spec\"; \"yeah make sure all of this custom shit is gone\". Owner, later the same day: \"no wire field shit\"; \"your main job is to read the hiccup and convert it into agent context. So you are defining your own render functions that need to work everywhere\"; \"The render functions are procesing real data schemas not made up processes\"; \"The hiccup is the driver but you in your render functions will be able to inject the current db and the rendered tree thus far in the recursvie render\"; \"if there's data missing or you need to agument it you can. You don't have to render everything\". And (owner, relayed by Web): \"if context runs on their own without the recursive render system in place (their renders are NOT a separate system!) they are just going to make a parallel system\". So there is no model walk: the one walk (seon.render/walker), reading for the model, calls each entity's schema model render (:seon.render/model), given {:db :so-far} (the db, the messages rendered before it) and the entity, answering :seon.model/message values or nothing; the request is those values in order.

Order: (1) stage 1a, attribute keys; (2) the model renders as plain functions, one per entity schema, declared on the schema (:seon.render/model): entity, db and what is rendered so far -> messages, proved as functions on my.test's world, with no traversal; (3) each turn's diff as the model call's model render, a function of the db at two bases; (4) the proof harness, test/seon/recorded_requests.edn byte for byte; then (5) Web's half of the one walk (the emit branch where a view of an entity calls its schema's render, with a reader option choosing html or model) lands with these functions plugged in, and every :wire reader is deleted in that change. The experiment's traversal over the hiccup was a lab stand-in and is not built. No :wire/* attribute on any element: the marks and every reader of them go. Each turn's diff is its model call's model render, between its :seon.model-call/basis and the previous call's. The places that recognize model data by hand-written key checks, each to go (Context, read on main 4531d84d48):
- seon.render/wire-parts: reads :wire/role, :wire/field, :wire/call-id, :wire/ref, :wire/turn, :wire/reply and :wire/result by key, a case over role strings, a literal set of field names, :seon.render/unknown-mark when one doesn't match: deleted, with every reader of the marks.
- seon.render/part-form and wire-parts' page check: deleted; the model reads each entity's model render.
- seon.render/page-lists and the request's list choice: the :seon.render/messages and :seon.render/list var metadata (a list counts as conversation because a var says so).
- seon.ctx.wire/request: the system-message check on the first message's role, shown? through render-eval?, the :example check, the appended whole-page call.
- seon.ctx.wire/render-eval? and render-calls: an eval recognized by matching its code text; deleted with seon.turn/show-page!.
- seon.ctx.wire/wire-turn's fresh?: the turn grouping becomes the model call's data.
- the other :wire reads in seon.render, swept here.
seon.web.html's stripping of :wire attributes when it writes html is Web's, deleted with the marks.

Settled 2026-11-09 by Web's one-walk spike (recorded in seon.render/an-entity-is-drawn-by-its-schemas-render; branch jolt/one-walk at b823b4c71b) and the owner (\"render both the hiccup and the agents context as the processing of the pull\"; \"No magic functions\"; \"I think this works even without the cache\"): there is no walk of the hiccup for the model. A model render is a plain function on its entity schema, (f {:seon.db/db db} entity), answering a vector of messages and the child entities it places, in its order; the walk runs it in the entity's component beside its hiccup render and places each child's output where it stands. Proved in the spike: a turn's diff is the model call's model render, the agent's page parts rendered at its :seon.model-call/basis and at the previous call's (each point's db made from its commit, the agent pulled there) and compared by seon.render/page-edits over seon.render/forms-moved, first in the call's messages, nothing stored; my.render/render-ctx takes (pull db) and answers {:hiccup :messages}, and a diff takes (db agent p0 p1).
Context's work, plugging into Web's mechanism once it lands: a model render per entity schema (the agent's system message from :seon.agent/system, a message, a model call with its turn's diff and its reply, an eval's call and result); the task is read only in the page parts, never a system message of its own (the spike's task render read the plan as it is now, so turn 1's message changed with the plan and the cached prefix broke); what the diff carries has no presentation (class, style, element ids), as seon.render/part-form leaves out today; a restart renders the chain of bases in one pass (seon.ctx.wire/diffs-through), not two cold renders a turn (about 2 x turns renders: 45M ticks at 160 turns by the spike's numbers); deletes seon.turn/show-page!, the stored page evals and seon.ctx.wire/render-calls, render-eval?, my.render/render-changes, the walk's :shown and seon.render/page-value, and every :wire reader above; my.render's functions take what they read; test/seon/recorded_requests.edn byte for byte, or each change named. The name of the schema property (:seon.render/model is taken by seon.render/model-declared) is the owner's.

