 {:seon.task/name "an-entity-is-drawn-by-its-schemas-render"
  :seon.task/plan "Stage 2a of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09 (owner, 2026-11-09: \"No layout-of. No seon.render/layout I want to remove all the manual layout work. A hiccup render can override anywhere we are walking and rendering recursively. It's just more data that we are detecting and rendering\"; \"Entities should have renders IFF we want them rendered\"). Needs stage 1a (a context keyed by attributes, on main) and seon.repl/an-agent-draws-its-own-page merged.

What's wrong: a page is drawn by hand-written layouts that decide in code which keys get parts and in what order (seon.render.ui/render-ctx, user-ctx, root-ctx, task-list, conversation-list, page-chrome's lists), named by :seon.render/layout and read by seon.render/layout-of; four entities have two renders (task: task-view and task/status/citations; model call: model-call-view and conversation-turn; eval: evaluation and eval-item/eval-glance; message: message and message-hiccup); a cite, a doc section, a search, a result and a var with its verdict have no render and are drawn inside layout code; a cite's pull reached back to its agent, drawn again (Web's lab, 2026-11-09).

The change: the walk's root is the pulled entity drawn by its render (its own :seon.render/hiccup, else its entity schema's); a render returns its data and the walk draws each entity it holds by that entity's render, one component each, keyed by its path (seon.render/emit: a tracking view of an entity placed in hiccup draws as its render's component, :at its path); an explicit [#'f {} x] stays for a true layout; a bare {:db/id} draws as a link to where the entity is, with a model render naming its identity value. One render per entity schema; the missing ones written; eval tiles: an eval whose result's value has a render (its own key, or :seon.eval/schema's) is drawn in the sidebar by the result's render. The page-or-conversation split is Context's model renders (stage 2b). Deletes: seon.render/layout-of, :seon.render/layout, the layouts and their lists, each second render. Proofs: a page is the user's (or an agent's) pull drawn with no layout var; a change to one entity reruns its component alone (seon.render-cost-test/a-change-costs-its-components, its bound measured before and moved only on the owner's yes); no entity is drawn twice; test/seon/recorded_requests.edn unchanged where the data is, else named part by part with Context.

Experiment A, 2026-11-09 (Web, a demo root on a clone of runs/store, lab renders over a ratom shaped like ctx: an agent, its task, three evals): a render that returns its data, with each view of an entity it holds turned into a call of that entity's schema's render, the view the call's argument (a six-line helper standing in for one branch of seon.render/emit; seon.ratom/view= makes the key stable), draws each entity as its own component keyed by its render and path, with no layout. Changing one eval's code reran that eval's component alone; the task's plan, the task's alone; an eval added, the new eval and the agent's list. Estimate held: O(the entity that changed), plus a lookup a member for the list it is in. So stage 2a's mechanism is that one branch of emit; the rest of the stage is deleting layouts and writing the missing renders.

The one-walk spike, 2026-11-09 (Web, branch jolt/one-walk at b823b4c71b, not for landing; src +140 -30). Owner: \"render both the hiccup and the agents context as the processing of the pull\"; \"The pull doesn't need to be in order the render functions for the data being processed can order them\"; \"look up what something is the same way our tx processor looks up and validates data\"; \"No magic functions\". Built and proved (tests one-walk-renders-a-pull-to-hiccup-html-and-messages and one-walk-a-turns-diff-is-rendered-not-stored, green):
- Recognition by seon.db/entity-schemas (the write check's index), and one lookup naming an entity's render for every output (entity-render in the spike).
- In seon.render/render!, an entity's model render runs after its hiccup in the same run, reads tracked together; its output holds messages and the child entities it places, each the child's component (the one the hiccup placed, a cache hit), kept as a marker; the request is the root's output with each marker replaced by its child's, as seon.render/resolved does for hiccup (two lines).
- The agent's hiccup and model renders both order the turns, from one helper.
- One eval changed reruns its component alone, page and model; a new turn on a live walk reruns 3 components (the agent, the call, the eval) and leaves the earlier messages the same bytes.
Measured (seon.cost/measure, test world): a new turn costs O(turns): 0.65M ticks at 10 turns, 2.29M at 40, 9.17M at 160 (one turn added and taken back); the renders, the message read and the ctx diff are a seventh of it, the rest the walk's cache-hit path checking each earlier child, twice (hiccup and model output). Rendered from nothing (no cache): 1.5M at 10, 5.7M at 40, 22.8M at 160 (about 70 ms). So the cache is never relied on; a cheaper hit path (no contracted calls, the second placement reusing the first's key) is a later cost task, measured on a real session first.
To do here, beyond the spike: recognition by the best match (attributes held per schema, inherited counted, a tie an error value) instead of seon.db/most-specific's extends-all rule; the (and (map? v) (:db/id v)) check goes (a map under a ref attribute is an entity: the attribute it came through says what it reaches, seon.db/targets); seon.db/parents, extends?, entity-schemas and their readers take the registry they read (the branch's, as seon.db/index-of gives the write check), where today they read the binary's while seon.contract/properties reads the branch's; renders named by data (a value's own :seon.render/hiccup, a schema's through seon.render/render-of) run as components, so seon.web.html/expand runs none and an eval's glance stops staying stale after its render is redefined; the spike's agent render becomes the agent's :seon.render/hiccup (seon.render.ui/agent's layout list goes).
It reds 11 tests: 10 because root's entity holds root's attributes and the seon.config.* keys (by the best match, the config): stage 3's config entity fixes the data; one, seon.render-test/one-path-to-the-model, holds the :wire parts' old law, which 2b deletes."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-user-agent-renders-the-users-view"
  :seon.task/plan "Stage 4 of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09, Web's part (Optimization's: the user agent's lifecycle and branch). Owner, 2026-11-09: \"I want the user to have a process regardless. Anything that's user focused should be handled by this agent too so separate users don't take down the system\"; \"it's a read only ctx tree. The user isn't modifying anything it's sending the requests to agents\"; \"root I think is mostly the go between for flow communication\".

The change: the user agent's walk of its pull (itself, root, the agents it started: :seon.agent/agents, their main half) is the person's whole view, drawn by the user's render (its home: root's chat in the middle and the session tiles beside it, owner 2026-11-08). For each agent its render shows whole, it asks that agent {:op :render :since t} and holds the answers in a ratom of its own beside @ctx, diffed as ctx is (seon.ctx/changes) and read through a view, so a component reruns on its own agent's paths; two agents reaching each other answer with each other's last render. Root's web proc serves the user agent's hiccup as html and Datastar patches, a malformed answer an error on that user's page; root runs no render. Opening an eval is an eval with origin :context the user agent sends the agent. What a person types is read by the user agent (seon.reader/words-and-forms) and sent to root or the agent the view is on. Deletes: seon.render.proc's user-walk and root-walk, its page passes over agents' branches, seon.root/opened!. Proofs: one pushed answer changes one component (measured in the lab 2026-11-09: 1.0 ms); a page open in two tabs is one render; the user agent's death leaves root and other users' pages up.

Experiment D, 2026-11-09 (Web, same demo root): the agents' renders the user agent holds, one ratom diffed by seon.ctx/changes (a sorted map by agent key, each a sorted map by component id), read in a component through seon.ratom/ref-view: no new code. One part changed reran that part and its agent's list (0.23 ms, 2 agents x 30 parts of about 400 bytes); a part added, the new part and its agent's list; a status moved on main, that agent's component alone; another agent's part, that agent's alone. Estimate: O(1) for the changed part plus a lookup per part of that one agent. Each answer's components need ids as keys (the key-id and data-rev of ``seon.repl/an-agents-process-renders-data-to-hiccup``)."
